Binary Search
Problem Statement
We need to find the position of a given element in a sorted array using the Binary Search algorithm.
If the element is present, we will return its index. Otherwise, we will return -1.
Approach
To do this we will follow these steps:
We will initialize two pointers:
low = 0
high = n - 1
We will calculate the middle index using
mid = low + (high - low) / 2
If the middle element is equal to the target, we will return its index.
If the target is greater than the middle element, we will search in the right half.
If the target is smaller than the middle element, we will search in the left half.
We will repeat this process until the element is found or the search space becomes empty.
Code
#include<iostream>
using namespace std;

int BinarySearch(int array[], int n, int target)
{
    int low=0;
    int high=n-1;

    while(low<=high)
    {
        int mid=low+(high-low)/2;

        if(array[mid]==target)
            return mid;

        else if(array[mid]<target)
            low=mid+1;

        else
            high=mid-1;
    }

    return -1;
}

int main()
{
    int array[]={2,4,6,8,10,12,14};
    int n=7;
    int target=10;

    cout<<BinarySearch(array,n,target);

    return 0;
}
Example
If we have the array
2 4 6 8 10 12 14
and
Target = 10
The output will be
4
This is because 10 is present at index 4 in the array.
Time Complexity
The time complexity of this solution is
O(log n)
This means that after every comparison, the search space is reduced to half, making Binary Search much faster than Linear Search for sorted arrays.
Space Complexity
The space complexity of this solution is
O(1)
